/* The Aligned content: Powerhouse Programme (from the master document) and the 66-Day challenge. */
const PROGRAMME = {"principles": [["Identity comes before strategy.", "A person must be rebuilt internally before any external plan will hold."], ["Purpose is found inside a problem", ", not invented in the abstract."], ["Character must catch up to gifting", "before real responsibility is safe to carry."], ["Skill and systems", "turn calling into repeatable competence."], ["Service under mentorship", "proves character and competence before any platform is given."], ["Voice follows proof", "— never the other way around."], ["Multiplication is the finish line", "— the process isn't complete until it reproduces in someone else."]], "facilitator": ["Run each course over 4 consecutive weeks; do not compress — the milestones are designed to require real time (field research, real conversations, a real project run) that can't be rushed.", "Do not allow a participant to move to the next course without the milestone deliverable from the current one in hand — the sequence only works cumulatively.", "Pair every participant with an accountability partner or small group by Course 3, Week 4 — this structure is load-bearing for the rest of the programme, not optional.", "Course 5 requires access to a real structure (an organization, ministry, or initiative) the participant can serve within — arrange this in advance where possible so Week 2 isn't a bottleneck.", "The programme closes with participants prepared to mentor someone else through Course 1 — consider how you'll support that multiplication step institutionally."], "closing": "Adelaja's own trajectory — and that of the people he transformed — did not end at personal success. It continued as multiplication: each person he raised up went on to raise up others. This course, and this programme, is only truly finished when you've done the same.", "courses": [{"n": 1, "title": "Identity & Purpose Discovery", "overview": ["This is the foundation course. Adelaja never moved a drug addict, ex-convict, or directionless young person straight into \"success strategies.\" He first dismantled the identity they were living from, and rebuilt it on purpose. Everything in later courses — problem-solving, systems, influence — sits on top of what gets built here."], "prereq": "", "principle": "Purpose precedes power. A person operating outside their purpose, however skilled, will plateau. A person who knows their purpose, however unskilled, becomes unstoppable.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "Confronting the Slave Mentality", "teaching": "Adelaja's starting diagnosis for every person he worked with — mafia member, addict, or ordinary person coasting through life — was the same: they were operating from a **slave mentality**, not a **master/steward mentality**. A slave mentality shows up as:\n- Waiting to be told what to do rather than initiating\n- Blaming circumstances, government, family, or luck for one's position\n- Believing significance is reserved for \"special\" people, not you\n- Working only for survival (a wage, a day-to-day existence) rather than for a mandate\n\nThe shift he demanded first was not behavioral — it was a change in **self-perception**. A person must stop seeing themselves as a victim of their story and start seeing themselves as a steward with an assignment. This is the same move he made with hardened addicts: he didn't lecture them about drugs first — he told them who they actually were underneath the addiction.", "exercises": [{"name": "Slave-mentality inventory", "text": "Write down 10 statements you commonly say or think about your life (\"I never get breaks,\" \"people like me don't...\"). Mark each as either steward-language or slave-language."}, {"name": "Origin audit", "text": "Write the story you've been telling yourself about why your life looks the way it does. Then rewrite it removing every external blame clause."}, {"name": "Daily practice", "text": "For 7 days, each morning state (aloud or written) one sentence: *\"I am not a victim of my circumstances; I am a steward of an assignment.\"* Track resistance — does it feel true or false, and why?"}], "milestone": "A written \"Before\" statement: one page describing your current self-image, honestly, including the excuses and limiting beliefs you've been operating from. This becomes your baseline — you'll compare against it at the end of the programme.", "mname": "\"Before\" Statement"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Discovering Your God-Given Gifting", "teaching": "Adelaja taught that every person is deposited with a gift or capacity before they are given an assignment — the gift is evidence of the calling, not separate from it. The mistake most people make is searching for purpose in the abstract (\"what am I passionate about?\") instead of examining what they've *already been given*: natural ability, recurring compliments from others, things that come easily to you that are hard for others, and what you find yourself doing without being asked.\n\nHe also stressed that gifting is often visible **before** it's refined — the raw material shows up early, in how you help people, what problems you naturally notice, what you were doing as a child without prompting.", "exercises": [{"name": "Gift-trace exercise", "text": "List every time in your life someone said \"you're really good at...\" or asked you for help with something specific. Look for a pattern."}, {"name": "Effortless competence list", "text": "Write 5 things that feel easy to you but that you've observed others struggle with."}, {"name": "", "text": "**Ask 3 people** who know you well (not family who might flatter you) what they think you're naturally gifted at, without explaining why you're asking. Record their answers without editing them."}, {"name": "Childhood recall", "text": "What did you gravitate toward before age 12, before anyone told you what you \"should\" want to do?"}], "milestone": "A one-page **Gift Profile**: 3–5 identified natural capacities, each backed by at least one piece of evidence from the exercises above.", "mname": "Gift Profile"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Purpose as Kingdom Assignment", "teaching": "This is the theological core of Adelaja's system. He taught that purpose is not self-invented — it is **discovered** and it is always oriented toward serving others, not merely self-fulfillment. He reframed life itself as an assignment given for the benefit of a specific sphere: your family, your street, your industry, your generation. This is what separates his model from generic self-help — purpose is never \"make yourself happy,\" it's \"what were you sent to fix, build, or carry for others.\"\n\nHe used the image of a farmer sowing seed: your gift is the seed, but it must be planted in a specific field (your assignment) to produce a harvest. Random effort without a defined field produces nothing.", "exercises": [{"name": "Sphere mapping", "text": "List the spheres you're already connected to (family, workplace, community, industry, faith community). For each, note one real, recurring pain point you observe."}, {"name": "Gift-to-sphere matching", "text": "Take your Gift Profile from Week 2 and match each gift to a sphere where it could be applied to a real need."}, {"name": "Draft assignment statement", "text": "Using the format: *\"I exist to bring [your gift] to [specific sphere/people] in order to [specific outcome].\"* Write 3 drafts, then narrow to one."}], "milestone": "A **Draft Purpose Statement** — one sentence, specific enough that a stranger could understand exactly who you serve and how. (This will be tested and refined in Course 2 against real problems.)", "mname": "Draft Purpose Statement"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Values Clarification & Character Audit", "teaching": "Before Adelaja let anyone build anything — a ministry, a business, a platform — he insisted on a character foundation. His conviction: gifting will take you further than your character can sustain you, and when that gap is exposed, the whole structure collapses. This is why rehabilitated addicts in his church were walked through rigorous, unglamorous character formation before being given any responsibility, however gifted they were.\n\nValues clarification here means identifying the non-negotiable principles that will govern your decisions once you're operating in your purpose — integrity, work ethic, honesty in dealings, treatment of people who can't benefit you. A character audit means honestly locating where your current conduct doesn't yet match those values.", "exercises": [{"name": "Values list", "text": "Write your top 7 non-negotiable values. For each, write one recent example where you lived it and one where you didn't."}, {"name": "Integrity gap audit", "text": "Identify 3 specific areas (financial habits, promises kept, honesty in small things) where there's a gap between who you say you are and how you actually operate."}, {"name": "Accountability step", "text": "Identify one person you will give permission to speak honestly into these gaps going forward. This person becomes part of your ongoing structure, not just this course."}], "milestone": "A **Personal Values Charter** — one page listing your 7 values, the gap next to each, and one concrete commitment per value for the next 90 days.", "mname": "Personal Values Charter"}], "deliverables": ["Your \"Before\" self-image statement", "Your Gift Profile (3–5 gifts with evidence)", "Your Draft Purpose Statement", "Your Personal Values Charter with 90-day commitments", "One accountability person identified"], "bridge": "These four documents are the raw material Course 2 (Problem-as-Purpose) will use — you cannot select the right problem to specialize in without first knowing your gifts, your sphere, and the character you're building from."}, {"n": 2, "title": "The Problem-as-Purpose Principle", "overview": ["This is Adelaja's signature doctrine, and the hinge on which his entire model turns: **significance is hidden inside a problem you are wired to solve.** He didn't tell rehabilitated addicts and marginalized Ukrainians to \"find their passion\" in the abstract — he pointed them at the pain around them and taught them that their pain, their outrage, and their compassion were pointing at their calling.", "This course takes the Gift Profile, Draft Purpose Statement, and Values Charter from Course 1 and puts them to work against real problems. By the end, you will have selected — and be able to defend — the specific problem-field you will build your life's work around."], "prereq": "Course 1 deliverables (Gift Profile, Draft Purpose Statement, Values Charter)", "principle": "A problem you're not personally provoked by will not sustain you through the difficulty of solving it. The right problem is the one you cannot walk past indifferently.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "The Problem-as-Purpose Doctrine", "teaching": "Adelaja's core reframe: most people run from problems, but he taught that **your platform is hidden inside the problem you're most bothered by.** He would point to how every great institution — a hospital, a school, a business, a ministry — exists because someone refused to accept a problem as permanent. The size of the problem you're willing to carry determines the size of the life you'll build.\n\nHe also taught a crucial distinction: not every problem is *yours*. Some problems provoke sympathy in everyone (poverty, injustice) but only a specific few are wired — by gifting, experience, and burden — to actually solve a given expression of that problem. Purpose is found at the intersection of a real problem and your specific capacity to address it.", "exercises": [{"name": "Provocation list", "text": "Write 10 things in the world (your community, industry, generation) that genuinely disturb, frustrate, or move you when you encounter them. Be specific, not generic (\"the way new teachers are left without mentorship\" not \"education is bad\")."}, {"name": "Frequency check", "text": "Circle the 3 items from your list that you find yourself thinking about, discussing, or trying to fix even when no one asked you to."}, {"name": "Cross-reference with Course 1", "text": "Next to each circled problem, note which of your identified gifts would actually apply to solving it."}], "milestone": "A **Provocation Shortlist**: 3 candidate problems, each with a one-paragraph explanation of why it disturbs you and which of your gifts applies to it.", "mname": "Provocation Shortlist"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Mapping the Problems That Provoke You", "teaching": "Adelaja pushed people to move from vague frustration to precise diagnosis. A problem stated generally (\"bad leadership\") cannot be solved; a problem stated precisely (\"first-time managers in small businesses have no structured training and fail within a year\") can be. He trained people to interrogate a problem the way an investigator would — who exactly is affected, how does it show up day to day, what has been tried and failed, and why does it persist.\n\nThis week is about taking your shortlist and subjecting each candidate problem to rigorous mapping, so you're choosing based on evidence and understanding, not just emotion.", "exercises": [{"name": "Problem anatomy", "text": "For each of your 3 shortlisted problems, answer: Who exactly suffers from this? How does it show up in daily life? What causes it to persist? What has already been tried?"}, {"name": "Proximity test", "text": "For each problem, rate your proximity to it on a scale (1 = you've only read about it, 5 = you've lived it or work inside it daily). Adelaja consistently pointed people toward problems they had proximity to."}, {"name": "Field research", "text": "Have at least 3 real conversations (not surveys) with people currently experiencing one or more of your shortlisted problems. Record what you learn, especially anything that surprises you."}], "milestone": "A **Problem Map** for each of your 3 shortlisted problems — one page each, covering who's affected, root causes, existing (failed) solutions, and your proximity score.", "mname": "Problem Map"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Complaint vs. Calling", "teaching": "Not every provocation is a calling — some are just complaints. Adelaja drew a sharp line between the two. A **complaint** talks about the problem, blames whoever is responsible, and stops there. A **calling** takes personal responsibility for being part of the solution, even when you didn't create the problem and it isn't \"your job\" to fix it. He often said the mark of a true assignment is that you feel compelled to act even without permission, funding, or a formal role — the burden itself pushes you to move.\n\nThis week tests your shortlist against that line: are you complaining about these problems, or are you actually willing to be responsible for them?", "exercises": [{"name": "Responsibility test", "text": "For each of your 3 problems, write honestly: have you only talked about this problem, or have you already taken any small action toward it (even informally)? List any past actions, however small."}, {"name": "The \"no permission\" test", "text": "Ask yourself for each problem: would I still try to do something about this if no one paid me, hired me, or gave me a title to do it? Be honest."}, {"name": "Cost audit", "text": "Solving a real problem costs something — time, money, reputation, comfort. For each shortlisted problem, name the actual cost you'd need to be willing to pay."}], "milestone": "Eliminate at least 1 of your 3 shortlisted problems using the Complaint vs. Calling tests. Write a short paragraph explaining why it was eliminated — this sharpens your discernment for the final decision.", "mname": "Elimination Record"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Selecting and Committing to Your Problem-Field", "teaching": "Adelaja was firm that transformation requires **commitment to a specific field**, not perpetual openness to everything. Scattered effort across many problems produces no depth and no reputation. He taught that once you've identified your problem through prayer, evidence, and honest testing, you plant your flag and go deep — this is what allows a person to become known, trusted, and eventually a thought leader in that field, because depth of focus is what produces mastery.\n\nThis final week is a decision week: you are not gathering more information, you are choosing.", "exercises": [{"name": "Final comparison", "text": "Lay your remaining 2 problems side by side against your Gift Profile, Purpose Statement, and Values Charter from Course 1. Which one is the strongest fit across all three?"}, {"name": "Write the case against yourself", "text": "For your leading choice, write the strongest argument for why you should NOT pursue this problem. If your commitment survives this, it's real."}, {"name": "Public declaration", "text": "Tell your accountability person (identified in Course 1) which problem-field you are committing to, and why. Saying it to another person converts a private decision into a real commitment."}], "milestone": "A **Problem-Field Commitment Statement** — one paragraph naming the specific problem you are committing to solve, who it affects, why you're positioned to address it, and what you're willing to sacrifice to pursue it. This statement becomes the anchor for every course that follows.", "mname": "Problem-Field Commitment Statement"}], "deliverables": ["Your Problem-Field Commitment Statement", "The eliminated problems and why (useful record of your discernment process)", "Field research notes from real conversations with affected people", "A named accountability person now aware of your commitment"], "bridge": "Course 3 (Mindset & Character Transformation) will now build the internal capacity — thinking patterns, discipline, and integrity — required to actually carry this problem-field toward mastery, rather than abandoning it when it gets difficult."}, {"n": 3, "title": "Mindset & Character Transformation", "overview": ["Adelaja's most controversial and most repeated insistence was this: **character precedes platform.** He watched gifted people destroy what they built because their thinking and their integrity hadn't grown to match their opportunity. Before he gave anyone in his church real responsibility — no matter how gifted or how committed to a problem — he put them through sustained character formation. This is why his rehabilitated addicts became business owners and civic leaders instead of relapsing: the internal architecture was rebuilt, not just the external behavior.", "This course rebuilds the way you think (from scarcity to stewardship), the integrity you operate with, and the discipline that sustains effort long after motivation fades. It is the least glamorous course in the programme and the one Adelaja considered most indispensable."], "prereq": "Course 2 deliverable (Problem-Field Commitment Statement)", "principle": "Your problem-field will eventually expose whatever is weak in your thinking, integrity, or discipline. This course closes those gaps before they cost you your assignment.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "Renewing the Mind: From Scarcity to Dominion", "teaching": "Adelaja taught that most people who feel stuck are not lacking opportunity — they are operating from a **poverty/scarcity mindset**: a belief that resources, opportunity, and favor are limited and mostly unavailable to them. He replaced this with what he called a **dominion or stewardship mindset**: the conviction that you have been given authority and responsibility over a sphere, and your job is to actively create and multiply value within it, not wait passively for conditions to improve.\n\nThis isn't positive thinking for its own sake — it's a practical shift. A scarcity thinker sees a lack of funding and stops. A dominion thinker sees a lack of funding as one obstacle to be solved among several, because the outcome is still their responsibility.", "exercises": [{"name": "Scarcity-language audit", "text": "For one week, track every time you think or say something rooted in scarcity (\"I can't afford to,\" \"there's no way,\" \"people like me don't get...\"). Write each one down as it happens."}, {"name": "Reframe drill", "text": "Take your 5 most common scarcity statements and rewrite each as a dominion statement: not denial of the difficulty, but ownership of the response (\"I can't afford to\" becomes \"I haven't yet found the resource path — what are three ways to get it?\")."}, {"name": "Obstacle inventory for your problem-field", "text": "List the 5 biggest obstacles standing between you and progress on your Problem-Field Commitment. For each, write one active step you could take this week, however small."}], "milestone": "A **Mindset Shift Log** — your 5 scarcity statements reframed into dominion statements, plus evidence of at least one active step taken this week on an obstacle you'd normally have treated as a stopping point.", "mname": "Mindset Shift Log"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Holiness and Integrity as the Foundation of Influence", "teaching": "Adelaja was direct about this: gifting will open doors that character cannot yet sustain, and when the gap between the two is exposed, it collapses everything built on top of it. He used \"holiness\" broadly — not just religious observance, but total alignment between private conduct and public claims: honesty in small dealings, keeping your word, how you treat people who can't benefit you, what you do when no one is watching.\n\nHe insisted this had to be addressed *before* someone was given a platform, not after — because a platform amplifies whatever is already true about a person, good or bad.", "exercises": [{"name": "Private-vs-public audit", "text": "Identify 3 areas where your private conduct doesn't fully match what you present publicly (financial honesty, promises made, how you speak about people behind their backs, etc.)."}, {"name": "Small-things test", "text": "Track for one week whether you keep small commitments: replying when you said you would, arriving when you said you would, doing what you said you'd do without being reminded. Note every miss."}, {"name": "Repair action", "text": "Choose one integrity gap from your audit and take a concrete corrective action this week — a conversation, a repayment, an apology, a changed habit."}], "milestone": "An **Integrity Charter Update** — revisit your Course 1 Values Charter, add specific evidence of where you closed a gap this week, and name one integrity practice you will maintain as a permanent discipline (e.g., \"I confirm every commitment in writing and honor it without reminders\").", "mname": "Integrity Charter Update"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Discipline: Work Ethic, Time Stewardship, and Delayed Gratification", "teaching": "Adelaja consistently pointed out that the difference between people with the same gifting and the same opportunity was discipline — the capacity to do the necessary, unglamorous work consistently, especially when it wasn't rewarded immediately. He taught time as a resource to be stewarded, not spent passively, and treated the ability to delay gratification (choosing long-term building over short-term comfort) as a core marker of readiness for real responsibility.\n\nThis is the week where mindset and integrity get converted into daily behavior — because none of the previous work matters without the discipline to act on it consistently.", "exercises": [{"name": "Time audit", "text": "Track your actual time use for 3 days (not your intended schedule — your real one). Identify where time is leaking away from your problem-field work."}, {"name": "Non-negotiable block", "text": "Establish one fixed, protected block of time each day (even 30–60 minutes) dedicated solely to work on your problem-field. Protect it for the full week regardless of motivation."}, {"name": "Delayed gratification drill", "text": "Identify one short-term comfort you regularly choose over long-term building (scrolling, procrastinating, spending). Replace it once daily this week with a deliberate action toward your problem-field."}], "milestone": "A **Discipline Log** — 7 consecutive days of your protected work block completed, with a one-line note each day on what was accomplished during it. Missed days must be logged too, with an honest reason — the goal is a truthful record, not a perfect one.", "mname": "Discipline Log"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Accountability Through Discipleship Structure", "teaching": "Adelaja never relied on willpower alone — his entire model was built on **small-group accountability structures (cell groups)**, not solo self-improvement. He believed transformation that isn't witnessed and reinforced by others doesn't hold under pressure. This is why addicts who went through his rehabilitation stayed transformed: they were embedded in a structure of people who knew their story and checked on their progress, not left alone with a set of resolutions.\n\nThis week formalizes that structure for you, converting the single accountability person from Course 1 into an ongoing rhythm, not a one-time conversation.", "exercises": [{"name": "Structure design", "text": "Define a recurring accountability rhythm (weekly or biweekly check-in) with your named accountability person. Decide what you will report each time: mindset shifts, integrity gaps, discipline log, and progress on your problem-field."}, {"name": "Full disclosure conversation", "text": "Have one honest conversation with your accountability person covering everything from this course: your scarcity patterns, your integrity gaps, your discipline log. Practice saying it out loud, not just writing it privately."}, {"name": "Expand the circle (optional but recommended)", "text": "Identify 1–2 additional people who share or support your problem-field commitment, and propose a small recurring group, mirroring the cell-group structure Adelaja used at scale."}], "milestone": "A **Standing Accountability Agreement** — a short written agreement with your accountability person(s): frequency of check-ins, what will be reported, and what happens if commitments are consistently missed. This structure carries forward through every remaining course.", "mname": "Standing Accountability Agreement"}], "deliverables": ["Mindset Shift Log (scarcity → dominion reframes)", "Updated Integrity Charter with a permanent integrity practice", "7-day Discipline Log", "Standing Accountability Agreement with at least one person"], "bridge": "Course 4 (Skill & System Building) will now take this internal foundation — mindset, integrity, and discipline — and use it to build real competence and replicable systems around your chosen problem-field. Without this course, skill-building tends to produce talented people who burn out or compromise; with it, skill compounds into mastery."}, {"n": 4, "title": "Skill & System Building", "overview": ["With purpose defined (Course 1), a problem chosen (Course 2), and internal character/discipline rebuilt (Course 3), this course turns to competence. Adelaja was explicit that passion and calling alone don't solve problems — skill does, and skill without a **system** to sustain it dies with the person who had it. His own book on this exact subject, *System Building: The Key to Resolving Every Problem and Attaining Every Goal*, argues that the people who \"rule the world\" are not the most gifted but the ones who learn to build repeatable systems around their gift.", "This course also addresses something Adelaja treated as inseparable from personal transformation: **economic self-sufficiency**. He moved people out of dependency and into business ownership as a matter of principle — a person is not fully free while financially dependent, and financial literacy is part of becoming a steward, not a slave."], "prereq": "Course 3 deliverables (Mindset Shift Log, Integrity Charter, Discipline Log, Accountability Agreement)", "principle": "A gift applied without a system produces a moment of impact. A gift applied through a system produces a lasting solution.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "Deliberate Skill Acquisition Mapped to Your Problem-Field", "teaching": "Adelaja rejected vague self-improvement (\"get better at things\") in favor of **targeted competence** — skill acquired specifically because your chosen problem requires it. He taught that once your assignment is clear, the question is no longer \"what should I learn?\" in the abstract, but \"what does solving this specific problem, for these specific people, actually require of me?\" This turns learning from a hobby into a mandate.\n\nHe also emphasized proximity to those already solving adjacent problems — learning from people ahead of you in the same field rather than trying to reinvent everything alone.", "exercises": [{"name": "Skill-gap map", "text": "Revisit your Problem-Field Commitment Statement (Course 2). List the 5 specific skills required to make real progress on it. Rate your current competence in each (1–5)."}, {"name": "Learning plan", "text": "For your 2 lowest-rated skills, identify one concrete learning resource or practice method you will use this week (a course, a mentor conversation, deliberate practice on a real task)."}, {"name": "Find a forerunner", "text": "Identify one person already solving a version of your problem (locally or more broadly). Study how they operate — their methods, their systems — even if you can't yet speak with them directly."}], "milestone": "A **Skill Development Plan** — your 5 required skills, current ratings, and a specific action already taken this week toward the 2 weakest ones.", "mname": "Skill Development Plan"}, {"n": 2, "title": "The System-Building Principle", "teaching": "This week is built directly on Adelaja's system-building doctrine. His central claim: most people fail not from lack of effort but from lack of **system** — a repeatable structure that produces a result reliably, independent of mood, motivation, or memory. A system converts a one-time win into a process that can be repeated, taught, and eventually run by others.\n\nHe taught a simple sequence for building any system: (1) identify the outcome you want repeated, (2) break down the steps that reliably produce it, (3) remove reliance on willpower by building the steps into routine/structure, (4) document it so it can be transferred.", "exercises": [{"name": "Outcome definition", "text": "Name one specific, repeatable outcome your problem-field work needs to produce (e.g., \"one new mentee trained per month,\" \"one community workshop delivered every quarter\")."}, {"name": "Step breakdown", "text": "Reverse-engineer the steps that would reliably produce that outcome, in order, as if you were writing instructions for someone else to follow."}, {"name": "Remove the willpower dependency", "text": "For each step, ask: does this rely on me remembering or feeling motivated? If yes, redesign it as a scheduled, triggered, or checklist-based action instead."}], "milestone": "A **Draft System Document** — one page describing your first repeatable system: the outcome, the ordered steps, and how each step is triggered rather than left to memory or mood.", "mname": "Draft System Document"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Building and Testing Your First System", "teaching": "Adelaja insisted systems be tested in real conditions, not theorized indefinitely. A system only proves itself when it's run once, under real constraints, and then corrected. This week moves from design to execution — running your draft system exactly once, in real conditions, and observing where it breaks or holds.\n\nHe also taught that the first run of any system is rarely the final version — the goal of a first attempt is not perfection but honest data about what needs to change.", "exercises": [{"name": "Run the system once", "text": "Execute your Draft System Document in real conditions this week, exactly as written, even if it feels rough."}, {"name": "Break-point log", "text": "Note every point where the system didn't work as planned: a step that took longer than expected, a resource you didn't have, a step you skipped under pressure."}, {"name": "Revise", "text": "Rewrite the system document incorporating what you learned. This is now Version 2."}], "milestone": "A **Tested System (v2)** — your system document after one real run, with a short \"lessons from the first run\" section documenting what changed and why.", "mname": "Tested System (v2)"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Financial Literacy and Economic Self-Sufficiency", "teaching": "Adelaja treated financial dependency as a form of the same slave mentality confronted in Course 1 — a person is not fully free to pursue their assignment while financially dependent on circumstances outside their control. This is why his rehabilitation model moved people toward business ownership and economic productivity, not just spiritual or psychological recovery. He taught financial literacy as basic stewardship: knowing where your money goes, separating giving/saving/investing from consumption, and building at least one income stream connected to your gifting and problem-field wherever possible.", "exercises": [{"name": "Financial clarity audit", "text": "Write down your current income sources, fixed obligations, and where discretionary money actually goes (not where you assume it goes — check real records)."}, {"name": "Stewardship structure", "text": "Define a simple structure for your money going forward: a fixed proportion for giving, saving/investing, and living expenses."}, {"name": "Income-to-purpose link", "text": "Identify one realistic way your problem-field work could eventually generate income (directly or adjacently), even if it starts small or part-time. Write the first concrete step toward testing this."}], "milestone": "A **Financial Stewardship Plan** — your current financial clarity audit, your stewardship proportions going forward, and one concrete first step taken toward an income stream connected to your problem-field.", "mname": "Financial Stewardship Plan"}], "deliverables": ["Skill Development Plan (5 mapped skills, action taken on the weakest 2)", "Tested System (v2) — a real, run-once-and-revised repeatable system", "Financial Stewardship Plan with an income-to-purpose link"], "bridge": "Course 5 (Practice Ground — Serving Before Leading) will now put your skills and system to work inside a real structure, under mentorship, proving faithfulness with small responsibilities before you're given a larger platform — the step Adelaja never allowed anyone to skip."}, {"n": 5, "title": "Practice Ground: Serving Before Leading", "overview": ["This is the step Adelaja never allowed anyone to skip, regardless of talent, education, or urgency to \"make an impact.\" His model consistently placed even highly gifted people into small, unglamorous service roles inside a real structure before ever giving them a platform. He did this deliberately — a person's character and competence are only proven under real conditions, with real people, doing work that offers no spotlight. Skipping this step is, in his model, the single most common reason gifted people fail once given real responsibility.", "This course takes your tested system and financial plan from Course 4 and puts them to work inside a real structure — not a simulation, not a private project, but a setting where your service is visible, evaluated, and accountable to others."], "prereq": "Course 4 deliverables (Skill Development Plan, Tested System v2, Financial Stewardship Plan)", "principle": "Faithfulness in small, unseen responsibilities is what qualifies a person for larger, visible ones — not the other way around.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "The Principle of Faithfulness in Small Things", "teaching": "Adelaja repeatedly taught that responsibility scales upward only from a foundation of faithfulness in small things — the person who cannot be trusted with a minor task will not magically become trustworthy with a major one. He deliberately started transformed addicts and ex-convicts on tasks with no status attached: cleaning, serving food, administrative work — not because the tasks mattered in themselves, but because how a person handles small, unglamorous responsibility reveals their true character under real conditions, not their stated intentions.\n\nThis week is about identifying where you currently stand on this measure, honestly, before entering a structure.", "exercises": [{"name": "Small-things audit", "text": "List 5 small responsibilities in your current life (commitments, chores, minor duties to others) and rate honestly how consistently you've handled each over the last month."}, {"name": "Status check", "text": "Write honestly: do you tend to give full effort only to tasks that are visible or rewarded? Identify one recent example either way."}, {"name": "Reset commitment", "text": "Choose one small, currently neglected responsibility and recommit to it fully this week, with no expectation of recognition."}], "milestone": "A **Faithfulness Baseline** — your small-things audit with honest ratings, plus evidence that you completed your reset commitment this week without being reminded.", "mname": "Faithfulness Baseline"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Finding and Entering a Structure to Serve Within", "teaching": "Adelaja's model was never solo — transformation happened inside a structure (his church, its ministries, its rehabilitation programmes) where a person could serve under real oversight. He taught that you don't build your platform in isolation; you enter an existing structure connected to your problem-field, take the smallest available role, and let your competence and character be tested there before anyone hands you more.\n\nThis week is about identifying and entering such a structure — not creating your own yet, and not waiting for the \"right\" role, but taking the entry point available now.", "exercises": [{"name": "Structure scan", "text": "Identify 3 existing organizations, groups, ministries, or initiatives already working on some version of your problem-field."}, {"name": "Entry-point identification", "text": "For each, find the smallest realistic way to contribute — volunteering, assisting, shadowing — not a leadership or paid role."}, {"name": "Make contact", "text": "Reach out to at least one of these structures this week and offer to serve in whatever small capacity is available, without negotiating for more."}], "milestone": "An **Entry Confirmation** — documentation that you have secured (or are actively pursuing) an actual small serving role inside a real structure connected to your problem-field.", "mname": "Entry Confirmation"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Serving Under Mentorship", "teaching": "Adelaja placed enormous weight on mentorship — not self-directed learning, but submission to someone further along who can correct, model, and vouch for you. He taught that a mentor sees blind spots you cannot see in yourself, and that the willingness to be corrected by someone more experienced is itself a character test. Within his structures, people were never left to interpret their role alone; someone more experienced was always present to guide and evaluate.\n\nThis week is about actively engaging mentorship inside the structure you've entered — not passively hoping for it, but seeking it out.", "exercises": [{"name": "Identify a mentor figure", "text": "Within your new serving role, identify someone more experienced who could speak into your work, even informally."}, {"name": "Ask for feedback", "text": "Request specific feedback on how you're performing your small role — not general encouragement, but honest correction."}, {"name": "Respond to correction", "text": "If you receive any correction this week (from your mentor figure or your Course 3 accountability partner), document how you responded — defensively or with genuine adjustment."}], "milestone": "A **Mentorship Engagement Log** — who your mentor figure is, what feedback you requested and received, and one concrete adjustment you made in response.", "mname": "Mentorship Engagement Log"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Real-World Problem-Solving Projects and Earning Trust", "teaching": "Once faithfulness in small things is demonstrated and mentorship is engaged, Adelaja's model allowed for slightly larger, still-supervised responsibility — a real project tackling a piece of the actual problem, inside the structure, with oversight. This is the proving ground where your Course 4 system gets applied to real people and real stakes for the first time, under supervision rather than alone. Trust is earned here incrementally, not requested.", "exercises": [{"name": "Propose a small project", "text": "Within your serving structure, propose one specific, bounded project that applies your Tested System (from Course 4) to a real piece of your problem-field, with your mentor figure's involvement or oversight."}, {"name": "Execute and document", "text": "Carry out the project, however small, and keep a record of what worked, what didn't, and how people responded."}, {"name": "Request an honest evaluation", "text": "Ask your mentor figure and accountability partner for a direct evaluation: has your reliability and character shown growth since Week 1's baseline?"}], "milestone": "A **Practice Ground Report** — a summary of the small project completed, honest feedback received from your mentor figure, and a comparison against your Week 1 Faithfulness Baseline showing concrete change (or honestly, lack of it, if that's the case).", "mname": "Practice Ground Report"}], "deliverables": ["Faithfulness Baseline and evidence of improvement", "Entry Confirmation into a real serving structure", "Mentorship Engagement Log", "Practice Ground Report from a completed real-world project"], "bridge": "Course 6 (Voice & Influence) will now take this proven track record — real service, real feedback, real results — and turn it into the foundation for your public voice. Adelaja was clear that influence built before proof is fragile; influence built on demonstrated faithfulness and results is what makes a thought leader credible rather than performative."}, {"n": 6, "title": "Voice & Influence", "overview": ["Adelaja taught that every genuine problem-solver eventually becomes a teacher — not because they seek a platform, but because people naturally turn to whoever has actually solved something for guidance. He was insistent, though, that this sequence cannot be reversed: voice must follow proof, not precede it. This is why the programme has waited until now — after purpose, problem, character, skill, and real service — to address public influence. A voice built before results is performance; a voice built on demonstrated results is authority.", "This course takes your Practice Ground Report from Course 5 and converts it into a credible public presence — writing, speaking, and content that extend your solution to more people than you could reach one at a time."], "prereq": "Course 5 deliverables (Faithfulness Baseline, Entry Confirmation, Mentorship Log, Practice Ground Report)", "principle": "Teach only what you have actually done. Your voice carries weight in proportion to the proof standing behind it.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "Every Solver Becomes a Teacher", "teaching": "Adelaja's model treats teaching not as a separate career choice but as the natural next stage of genuine problem-solving — once you've walked through a real difficulty and produced a real result, you carry something others facing the same problem need. He warned against two opposite errors: hiding your results out of false humility, and broadcasting opinions you haven't actually lived. The mandate to teach is earned by the work already done in Courses 1–5, not assumed in advance.", "exercises": [{"name": "Proof inventory", "text": "List everything you can now say you have actually done, from Courses 1–5: the problem you committed to, the system you built and tested, the real project you completed, the feedback you received."}, {"name": "Teachable-moment extraction", "text": "For each item in your proof inventory, write one specific lesson someone else facing the same problem would benefit from knowing."}, {"name": "Audience identification", "text": "Name who specifically needs to hear these lessons — people a step or two behind you in facing the same problem, not a generic \"everyone.\""}], "milestone": "A **Teaching Inventory** — 5–7 specific, proof-backed lessons you are now qualified to teach, each tied to a real experience from earlier courses, plus a named target audience.", "mname": "Teaching Inventory"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Documenting Your Journey and Results", "teaching": "Adelaja's own influence was built substantially through documentation — books, sermons, recorded teaching — turning lived experience into transferable material. He treated documentation as a discipline, not an afterthought: capturing what happened, what was learned, and what worked, close to when it happened, rather than relying on memory later. This week builds the habit and the raw material.", "exercises": [{"name": "Journey timeline", "text": "Write a clear, honest timeline of your transformation so far: where you started (Course 1's \"Before\" statement), the problem you chose, the obstacles in building your system, and the real project from Course 5."}, {"name": "Results documentation", "text": "Gather any concrete evidence of impact from your Practice Ground project — feedback, outcomes, changes observed — organized clearly."}, {"name": "Ongoing documentation habit", "text": "Establish a simple weekly practice (a note, a voice memo, a journal entry) of recording what you're learning and doing in your problem-field, starting this week and continuing forward."}], "milestone": "A **Documented Journey** — a written account of your transformation and results so far, plus a committed ongoing documentation habit already in practice for one week.", "mname": "Documented Journey"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Finding and Using Your Medium", "teaching": "Adelaja used multiple mediums — preaching, writing, radio, and later mass publishing — but always matched the medium to what he was actually good at communicating, rather than forcing himself into a format that didn't fit. He taught that the medium is secondary to the substance; the goal is choosing whichever form (writing, speaking, teaching, mentoring conversations) most naturally and clearly carries your proof-backed lessons to your named audience.", "exercises": [{"name": "Medium inventory", "text": "List the ways you naturally communicate well (writing, speaking one-on-one, teaching groups, structured conversation) based on real past experience, not aspiration."}, {"name": "Match to audience", "text": "Considering your Week 1 target audience, decide which medium would most naturally reach and serve them."}, {"name": "First piece", "text": "Create one piece of teaching content in your chosen medium, drawn directly from your Teaching Inventory — a short piece of writing, a recorded talk, a structured lesson delivered to a small group."}], "milestone": "A **First Teaching Piece** — one completed, proof-backed piece of content in your chosen medium, delivered or published to at least a small real audience (not left in draft).", "mname": "First Teaching Piece"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Building a Credible Public Presence", "teaching": "Adelaja's influence grew from consistency over time, not a single viral moment — regular teaching, delivered reliably, compounding into trust and reach. He also insisted that public presence remain tethered to real substance: the goal was never visibility for its own sake, but making genuine solutions available to more people. This final week establishes a sustainable rhythm rather than a one-off effort.", "exercises": [{"name": "Consistency plan", "text": "Define a realistic, sustainable rhythm for producing teaching content going forward (e.g., one piece every two weeks), sized to what you can actually sustain alongside your problem-field work."}, {"name": "Presence audit", "text": "Identify the one or two platforms or settings (a blog, a local group, a speaking venue, a mentoring circle) where your target audience is most likely to actually encounter your teaching."}, {"name": "Accountability integration", "text": "Share your consistency plan with your Course 3 accountability partner and agree on how they'll check you're sustaining it."}], "milestone": "A **Voice & Influence Plan** — your sustainable content rhythm, chosen platform(s), and accountability check-in arrangement, with your Week 3 first piece as evidence the rhythm has already begun.", "mname": "Voice & Influence Plan"}], "deliverables": ["Teaching Inventory (5–7 proof-backed lessons)", "Documented Journey with results evidence", "First Teaching Piece, delivered to a real audience", "Voice & Influence Plan with a sustainable rhythm"], "bridge": "Course 7 (Multiplication & Legacy) will now take your voice and turn it outward — mentoring others through the same process, and building the structure or institution that allows your transformation to reproduce in others, which is how Adelaja measured lasting impact."}, {"n": 7, "title": "Multiplication & Legacy", "overview": ["This is the final course, and in Adelaja's model, the true measure of transformation. He was explicit that a person is not fully transformed until they can reproduce that transformation in others — his own church multiplied precisely because everyone who was helped was trained to help someone else the same way, creating a self-sustaining structure rather than permanent dependency on him personally. A powerhouse who cannot multiply themselves remains a talented individual; a powerhouse who multiplies becomes a movement.", "This course takes everything built across Courses 1–6 — your purpose, your problem-field, your character, your system, your service record, your voice — and turns it outward, toward reproducing the same process in others and building something that outlasts your personal involvement."], "prereq": "Course 6 deliverables (Teaching Inventory, Documented Journey, First Teaching Piece, Voice & Influence Plan)", "principle": "You have not finished the process until someone else has gone through it because of you.", "weeks": [{"n": 1, "title": "The Multiplication Mandate", "teaching": "Adelaja taught that the final test of any genuine transformation is reproducibility — can what happened to you happen to someone else because you were involved? He resisted models where influence centered permanently on one gifted individual, insisting instead that every person who received help be trained to extend it. This week establishes the mindset shift: from being the person who solves the problem to being the person who equips others to solve it too.", "exercises": [{"name": "Reproducibility check", "text": "Review your Tested System (Course 4) and Practice Ground Report (Course 5): could someone else follow what you did and get a similar result? Identify any gaps that exist only because they depend entirely on you personally."}, {"name": "Candidate identification", "text": "Name 2–3 people in your life or sphere who are where you were at the start of this programme — with potential but no clear direction — and who you could realistically walk through this same process."}, {"name": "Willingness audit", "text": "Write honestly: what holds you back from investing in someone else right now (time, fear of being outgrown, not feeling \"ready\")? Address the strongest objection directly."}], "milestone": "A **Multiplication Readiness Statement** — your reproducibility check, named candidates, and an honest resolution of your strongest objection to investing in others now.", "mname": "Multiplication Readiness Statement"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Mentoring Others Through the Process", "teaching": "Adelaja didn't teach mentorship as informal encouragement — it was structured, following the same stages he'd walked people through himself: identity and purpose, problem, character, skill, service, voice. He expected those he raised up to mentor with the same rigor and structure they experienced, not a diluted version. This week begins actual mentorship, using this same seven-course framework as your guide.", "exercises": [{"name": "Select one candidate", "text": "From your Week 1 list, approach one person and invite them into a mentoring relationship, explaining honestly what it will involve (time, honesty, structure)."}, {"name": "Begin at Course 1", "text": "Walk your mentee through the Week 1 exercises of Course 1 (Confronting the Slave Mentality) this week, in your own words, drawing on your own experience of it."}, {"name": "Reflect on the reversal", "text": "Note what it feels like to be on the teaching side of exercises you once did yourself. Where do you find yourself explaining it differently than how you experienced it?"}], "milestone": "A **Mentorship Launch Record** — confirmation that you've begun mentoring one real person through Course 1, with notes on their initial response and your own reflections on teaching it.", "mname": "Mentorship Launch Record"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Building an Institution or System Others Can Plug Into", "teaching": "Adelaja moved deliberately from personal mentorship to institution-building — structures (ministries, training programmes, businesses) that could function and multiply even without his direct involvement in every instance. He taught that legacy requires moving beyond one-to-one mentoring toward systems that can onboard, train, and release many people at once. This is the same system-building principle from Course 4, now applied at a larger scale: not a system for solving the problem yourself, but a system for producing more solvers.", "exercises": [{"name": "Structure sketch", "text": "Sketch a simple structure (a programme, a small organization, a recurring training cohort) that could take more than one person at a time through a version of this transformation process in your problem-field."}, {"name": "Roles beyond you", "text": "Identify what parts of this structure could eventually be run by someone other than you — this is what determines whether it can actually multiply or remains dependent on you alone."}, {"name": "Smallest viable version", "text": "Define the smallest real version of this structure you could start now (even a single small cohort of 2–3 people) rather than waiting for an ideal, fully-resourced version."}], "milestone": "An **Institution Blueprint (v1)** — your structure sketch, the roles that could be delegated, and the smallest viable version you're prepared to start.", "mname": "Institution Blueprint (v1)"}, {"n": 4, "title": "From Personal to Societal Transformation, and Programme Capstone", "teaching": "Adelaja's ultimate aim was never individual success — it was societal transformation, one changed person eventually reshaping their family, community, industry, and nation. He measured impact generationally: not \"did I succeed\" but \"what continues after me, and how far does it reach beyond people I'll ever personally meet.\" This final week zooms out from your personal case to the wider sphere your problem-field sits within, and closes the programme with a full capstone review.", "exercises": [{"name": "Sphere-level vision", "text": "Write one page describing what meaningful change in your broader sphere (industry, community, generation) would look like if your Institution Blueprint succeeded and multiplied over 5–10 years."}, {"name": "Full programme review", "text": "Revisit every milestone document from Courses 1–7 (your \"Before\" statement, Purpose Statement, Problem-Field Commitment, Mindset/Integrity/Discipline logs, Tested System, Practice Ground Report, Teaching Inventory, Multiplication Readiness Statement). Write a candid \"Before and After\" comparison."}, {"name": "Commitment renewal", "text": "With your accountability structure from Course 3, formally renew your commitments: to your problem-field, to ongoing mentorship, and to building your Institution Blueprint further."}], "milestone": "A **Transformation Capstone Document** containing:\n1. Your Before/After comparison across all seven courses\n2. Your Sphere-Level Vision (5–10 year view)\n3. Your Institution Blueprint (v1)\n4. Renewed commitments witnessed by your accountability partner(s)\n\nThis document marks the formal completion of the programme — not because the work is finished, but because you are now equipped to continue it, and to walk others through the same seven courses that took you from an ordinary person with no direction into a problem-solver, a builder, and a thought leader in your field.", "mname": "Transformation Capstone Document"}], "deliverables": ["Before/After comparison across all seven courses", "Sphere-Level Vision (5–10 year view)", "Institution Blueprint (v1)", "Renewed commitments witnessed by your accountability partner(s)"], "bridge": ""}]};
